<BaseLayout title="Accumulation Conveyors: Zero-Pressure &#x26; Minimum-Pressure Buffer Systems" description="Accumulation conveyors: zero-pressure (ZPA), minimum-pressure, and slug-mode. Buffer sizing formulas, zone control technology, and packaging line integration." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema, articleSchema]}>
<h1>Accumulation Conveyors: Zero-Pressure &amp; Minimum-Pressure</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">An accumulation conveyor buffers products between upstream and downstream processes, absorbing speed differences, equipment stoppages, and operator breaks without shutting down the entire packaging line. Accumulation conveyors operate in three modes: zero-pressure (ZPA), minimum-pressure, and slug-mode. They use zone-controlled roller or belt drive systems to stop, hold, and release products on demand, preventing the product damage and line inefficiency caused by uncontrolled backpressure.</p>
<h2 id="why-heading">Why Accumulation Matters</h2>
<p>Every <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging line</a> consists of multiple machines operating at different speeds with different reliability profiles. A filler and a labeler rarely run at exactly the same rate. A case packer pauses while it forms each case. A palletizer runs in cycles. Without accumulation, any downstream stop forces an immediate upstream stop, cascading back through the entire line.</p>
<p>Accumulation conveyors decouple these processes. Products queue in a controlled buffer zone, and upstream machines continue producing while downstream equipment catches up. This decoupling raises overall line efficiency (OEE), because short downstream stops no longer stop the upstream machines. The buffer absorbs the micro-stops, speed differences, and changeovers that otherwise cascade into full-line shutdowns. For dedicated FIFO and LIFO buffer strategies, see our <a href="/conveyor-types/buffer-conveyors/">buffer conveyor guide</a>.</p>
<h3>The Backpressure Problem</h3>
<p>Without proper accumulation technology, products pile up on standard conveyors with full backpressure. The lead product presses against the stopped downstream machine. Every upstream product presses against the one in front of it. This causes label scuffing, carton crushing, shrink-wrap damage, and product shifting inside cases. Backpressure also overloads conveyor motors, trips drives, and increases belt and roller wear.</p>
<p>Accumulation conveyors solve this by controlling each zone independently, eliminating or minimizing the pressure between products.</p>
<h2 id="modes-heading">Three Accumulation Modes</h2>
<h3>Zero-Pressure Accumulation (ZPA)</h3>
<p>ZPA stops each product in its own zone with a controlled gap to the next product. Hytrol defines <a href="https://hytrol.com/products/accumulation/zero-pressure-conveyor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">zero-pressure conveyors</a> as accumulating conveyors that create space between products and do not allow them to touch. No product-to-product contact occurs at any point during accumulation. Each zone uses a photo-eye sensor to detect product presence and an independent drive mechanism to start or stop rollers in that zone. When a downstream zone signals "occupied," the upstream zone holds its product and sends an "occupied" signal further upstream.</p>
<p>ZPA protects fragile products, printed labels, shrink-wrapped packs, and any product where surface contact during accumulation causes damage. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> provide the cleanest ZPA implementation because each zone has its own motor. Belt-driven, line-shaft, and chain-driven live roller conveyors also come in zero-pressure versions, using pneumatic zone hardware or a gearmotor per zone.</p>
<h3>Minimum-Pressure Accumulation</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/minimum-pressure/">Minimum-pressure accumulation</a> allows light product-to-product contact. Hytrol describes <a href="https://hytrol.com/products/accumulation/minimum-pressure-conveyor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">minimum-pressure conveyors</a> as accumulation conveyors that allow products to lightly touch, with drive pressure on the tread rollers adjusted manually to bring backpressure to 2% of the live load. Products queue touching each other but with controlled force that suits durable goods like corrugated cases and rigid totes. Minimum-pressure systems need fewer sensors and zone controls than ZPA, which lowers cost.</p>
<p>On a belt-driven minimum-pressure conveyor such as Hytrol's 190-ACC, pressure rollers hold the drive belt against the carrying rollers, and the installer sets that pressure just high enough to move the heaviest product. When the lead product stops, the rollers under the queue slip, so each product pushes on the next with only light force. Hytrol builds both zero-pressure and minimum-pressure versions of its <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">powered roller conveyor</a> lines.</p>
<h3>Slug-Mode Accumulation</h3>
<p>Slug-mode accumulation groups products into batches (slugs) that release together. Slug size is set by the downstream process. Slug accumulation suits batch-oriented downstream processes like <a href="/conveyor-functions/tray-loading/">tray loading</a>, case packing, palletizer layer forming, and <a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">collating and laning operations</a>.</p>
<p>Slug zones release entire groups simultaneously on command from a PLC. The conveyor runs at transport speed between releases and holds the slug at rest between cycles. Zero-pressure zone controllers can often run in slug mode as well: Hytrol's CREZD manual describes switching its zone controllers between singulation mode and slug mode.</p>
