<h2 id="sizing-heading">How to Size Accumulation Buffer</h2>
<p>Buffer length determines how long the upstream line can continue running while downstream equipment is stopped. Undersizing the buffer eliminates the decoupling benefit. Oversizing wastes floor space and capital.</p>
<h3>Buffer Length Formula</h3>
<p class="formula"><strong>Buffer Length (ft) = (Products/min x Product Length in ft x Buffer Minutes) + (Gaps x Number of Products)</strong></p>
<p>Worked example with illustrative inputs: ZPA with 3" gaps between 12" cases at 30 cases per minute and 3 minutes of buffer. On a ZPA conveyor the actual gap depends on zone length, so use your quoted zone length minus product length.</p>
<p class="formula">Buffer = (30 x 1.0 x 3) + (0.25 x 90) = 90 + 22.5 = <strong>112.5 ft</strong></p>
<h3>How Much Buffer Time?</h3>
<p>Buffer time depends on how often the downstream machine stops and how long each stop lasts. Size from measured stop data on the existing line or from the machine supplier's stated changeover and replenishment times, then decide which stops the buffer must absorb. Typical stop causes by location:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Before a palletizer:</strong> sheet feeding, pallet dispensing, and pallet change cycles.</li>
<li><strong>Before a case packer:</strong> case blank replenishment and minor jams.</li>
<li><strong>Before a labeler or printer:</strong> ribbon and label roll changes.</li>
<li><strong>Before a manual station:</strong> operator breaks, shift changes, and task time variability.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Floor Space Alternatives</h3>
<p>Long accumulation runs consume floor space. Two alternatives reduce the footprint. Spiral accumulators wrap the accumulation conveyor into a vertical helix, trading vertical space for floor space. Bi-level accumulation uses an upper and lower conveyor tier connected by incline and decline sections, adding a second layer of accumulation over the same floor area. Both alternatives increase cost significantly compared to single-level accumulation.</p>
<h2 id="tech-heading">Accumulation Conveyor Technologies</h2>
<h3>MDR Zero-Pressure Accumulation</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR (Motor Driven Roller) conveyors</a> provide the cleanest ZPA implementation. Each zone contains a low-voltage DC motorized roller, idler rollers driven from it by bands or belts, and a photo-eye sensor. The zone controller reads the sensor, communicates with adjacent zones, and drives the motor. No compressed air, no pneumatic valves, and no mechanical clutches. MDR zones are a common building block in <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated systems</a>.</p>
<h3>Pneumatic BDLR Accumulation</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">Belt-driven live roller</a> zero-pressure accumulators remove drive from each zone pneumatically. In Hytrol's <a href="https://cdn.hytrol.com/2013_647_abez.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ABEZ</a>, each zone has a zone controller, a pressure frame that applies and removes drive, and a pneumatically operated brake. The ABEZ manual sets the air regulator to a working pressure of 35 psi. This approach needs compressed air and a valve in each zone.</p>
<h3>Line-Shaft Accumulation</h3>
<p>Line-shaft accumulators add a stopping mechanism to each zone while the shaft keeps turning. In Hytrol's <a href="https://cdn.hytrol.com/2006_568_138nspez_190nspez.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">190-NSPEZ</a>, air bags in each zone inflate to stop that zone from driving, working with brake pads, and the manual sets the regulator to 40 psi. Without zone control, O-ring slip on the shaft gives light-pressure accumulation for lighter products. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/live-roller-conveyors/">live roller conveyor guide</a> for line-shaft details.</p>
<h3>Chain-Driven Live Roller Accumulation</h3>
<p>Pallets and heavy loads accumulate on chain-driven live roller with a gearmotor in each zone. In Hytrol's <a href="https://cdn.hytrol.com/2015_661_crezd.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CREZD models</a>, each zone has a zone controller, a zone starter box, and its own gearmotor, so pallets queue without touching and without compressed air. See <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR conveyors</a>.</p>
<h3>Belt Conveyor Accumulation</h3>
<p>Belt conveyors can provide accumulation for irregularly shaped products that cannot ride on rollers. Servo-driven zone belt conveyors use individual belt sections with independent drives. Each belt section operates like an MDR zone but with a continuous belt surface, which suits small products in packaging applications. Belt accumulation costs more per foot than roller accumulation but handles products that rollers cannot.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Accumulation Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Pre-palletizer buffering:</strong> The most common accumulation application in packaging. Cases accumulate before the palletizer so the upstream case packing line continues running during pallet changes, sheet insert cycles, and stretch wrapping. Size this buffer from the palletizer's measured stop durations.</p>
<p><strong>Between filling and packing:</strong> Filled containers accumulate between the filler/capper and the case packer. This absorbs the case packer's stop-and-go cycle and prevents the filler from shutting down during case forming delays.</p>
<p><strong>Before <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">quality inspection and reject</a>:</strong> Products accumulate before checkweighers, metal detectors, and vision inspection stations. The buffer ensures a steady, controlled feed rate into inspection equipment for reliable detection.</p>
<p><strong>Distribution center merge points:</strong> Products from multiple pick zones accumulate before merging onto trunk conveyor lines. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR ZPA zones</a> prevent products from colliding at merge points and ensure orderly release onto the main line.</p>
<p><strong>Before <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfer</a> and sortation:</strong> Products accumulate before right-angle transfers, diverters, and sorting stations. The accumulation zone meters products into the transfer at the correct spacing and timing for reliable transfer operation.</p>
<p><strong><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food and beverage lines</a>:</strong> ZPA accumulation protects labeled bottles, shrink-wrapped multipacks, and decorated cans from scuffing during line stops. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Sanitary accumulation conveyors</a> use stainless steel MDR rollers and open-frame construction for washdown environments.</p>
